1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,590
7:42 PM. The loudest train in the city.

2
00:00:05,050 --> 00:00:07,870
Around 90 decibels of steel on steel.

3
00:00:08,110 --> 00:00:10,150
Then she presses one button.

4
00:00:11,450 --> 00:00:14,450
And the train disappears. But nothing blocked it.

5
00:00:14,490 --> 00:00:16,610
Her earbuds added more sound.

6
00:00:17,250 --> 00:00:19,050
Here's how.

7
00:00:19,850 --> 00:00:26,110
On the outside, a tiny microphone hears the train, thousands of times every second.

8
00:00:26,650 --> 00:00:30,710
A chip takes that sound wave, and flips it upside down.

9
00:00:31,250 --> 00:00:33,970
Where the train pushes the air, the speaker pulls.

10
00:00:34,030 --> 00:00:36,470
Where it pulls, the speaker pushes.

11
00:00:37,250 --> 00:00:41,730
Two waves, exactly opposite. Added together, they cancel out.

12
00:00:43,050 --> 00:00:48,190
A second microphone, inside, listens to what's left, and fixes the mistakes.

13
00:00:49,050 --> 00:00:52,550
It works best on steady rumble, like engines and trains.

14
00:00:53,050 --> 00:00:57,390
Sudden sounds are harder, so the soft ear tip blocks those.

15
00:00:58,450 --> 00:01:02,070
So when she hears nothing, her ears are full of sound.

16
00:01:02,450 --> 00:01:04,910
Just the exact opposite of the train.

17
00:01:06,450 --> 00:01:08,250
Next stop: hers.

